section.center(data-transition="fade")
	h1 FsFx
	h3 for Reveal.js
	button.bigbutton.fsbutton(data-fs-gonext="1500") Start the show!
	.fsfx-disabled
		p.warning Note: this device does not support the Fullscreen API.

section.center(data-transition="zoom-in fade-out")
	h3 FsFx.js
	p.small Can do a few things:
	ol
		li Enter or exit fullscreen
		li Trigger 'next slide' after fullscreen
		li Toggle a class on any element if fullscreen
		li Auto-generate a fullscreen button
	p.small If a device does not support the Fullscreen API, a class of <code>no-fsfx</code> is added to the body. Use it to show warnings, hide elements, and so on.

section.center
	h4 1. Enter or exit fullscreen
	ul
		li Triggers if an element with a certain class is clicked
		li This baseclass is <code>fsbutton</code> by default
		li The baseclass can be changed in the plugin options

section.center
	h4 2. Trigger 'next slide' after fullscreen
	p.small The start button on the first slide of this deck does exactly that
	ul
		li If wanted, a 'next slide' function can be called
		li It can be added with a <code>data-fs-gonext="*"</code> attribute
		li The wildcard is the delay in milliseconds

section.center
	h4 3. Toggle a class on any element in fullscreen
	ul
		li A class toggle can be added with <code>data-fs-toggle="*"</code>
		li The wildcard is the class that is toggled in fullscreen
		li The Reveal element always gets the <code>fullscreen</code> class
		li.fsfx-enabled In this presentation, see the changes in fullscreen: the background and the button icons
	button.bigbutton.fsbutton.icon-fs(data-fs-toggle="icon-fsexit") Toggle fullscreen

section.center(data-background="white")
	h4 4. Auto-generate a fullscreen button
	ul
		li FsFx can generate a fullscreen button in your presentation <span class="fsfx-enabled">(like in this one, top right)</span>
		li This is only done if no such button exists outside the slides
		li The styling is included; colour and position can be tweaked
